## Round 1 evidence checkpoint (scientific review accepted)

Frozen brief committed before search: 6eead17a. Report and matrices:
`docs/jetp-audits/0735/report.md`, `landmark-coverage.csv`,
`operation-coverage.csv`, `acquisition-log.csv`, `archive-manifest.json`,
`local-input-manifest.json` in the same directory.

20/20 acquisition units; no expansion. June 2010 and June 2020 original rosters
support listed membership, including unfinished projects; June 2015 remains
unlocated and June 2021 inaccessible. Three HTTP archives failed DNS: no original
byte hashes invented. Existing local discovery inputs are hashed. Dates and
missing endpoints are explicit; zero original approval/signature pairs.

The 2010 roster removes entries after signing OR withdrawal. That rule is not
verified for 2020; listed presence cannot prove pending-signature state. Current
snapshot status is separate from historical membership. Triad retained as
unverified protocol candidates; no causal design selected. Ticket stays open
until PR acceptance; underlying archive, frame and endpoint gaps remain visible.

## Independent review

All five documentary exit criteria are addressed as a bounded evidence audit;
this does not establish a usable causal design or complete historical population.
An independent scientific reviewer accepted the report and matrices. Fast gate:
1549 passed, ten skipped; adherence:330 passed, thirteen skipped. The report
records the missing-DVC environment failure and successful hydrated rerun.
Ticket remains open until the report PR is accepted.

## Author-directed second pass, 14 September 2026

The author requests broader coverage beyond World Bank, energy and JETP
countries. Execute the frozen round-2 brief at
`docs/jetp-audits/0735/round2/brief.md`: ADB, AfDB, EIB and AFD routes,
all-sector catalogues and multiple non-JETP borrower strata. This replaces
the first round's single-lender restriction for the new pass only.

Additional acceptance: institution-by-stage availability matrix; documented
non-energy/non-JETP examples; actual milestone pairs where available; explicit
historical-population and dropped/cancelled coverage; costed next acquisition
priorities. Sparse findings remain valid, but narrow WB results are not taken
as evidence about the wider source landscape. No design is selected.

## Second-pass handoff

Current report: `docs/jetp-audits/0735/round2/report.md`. Three institutional scouts
used30units each; parentverification used12units. ADB andEIB originals demonstrate
paired lifecycle dates. The officialAFD bulk export contains2080financing IDs,
1492project IDs and1911award/signature pairs across sectors and borrower countries.
Loan/grant strata, multi-country rows, dated2024metadata, missing values and
reversed chronology remain explicit. AfDB individual leads remain unverified.

The prioritized next acquisition is AFD historical inclusion/retention rules and
an older snapshot, then original exceptions behind the seven reversed award/
signature pairs. Approved-but-unsigned coverage is needed for that stage; the
signed-to-first-payment route needs unpaid-contract retention checks. ADB/EIB
provide complementary milestone and population probes. Continue country/sector
exposure work under0736; no causal design is selected by this broader audit.

Eight original payloads are DVC-tracked in a separate provisional audit bundle.
Remote replication failed (padme connection refused); all nine cache objects are
preserved in the primary local cache. The canonical source archive was not
rewritten from its partial checkout. No website or ledger data changed.

## Author-directed bilateral pass, 14 September 2026

Round 3 extends AFD to current XML/portal reconciliation and adds JICA, KfW,
USAID and UK FCDO. Report: `docs/jetp-audits/0735/round3/report.md`.
91 bounded external units, original payload checks and source manifests.

AFD current portal has 3,277 financing IDs; fixed-country XML retains 223 of
224 legacy IDs, including completed and payment-missing records, but loses
CMA123501. Full flat-table comparison has 19 missing IDs and two signature
discrepancies. Exact legacy first-payment dates do not match earliest periodic
XML transactions. KfW/BMZ supplies 4,036 activities with coarsened payment dates;
FCDO supplies 7,327 programmes plus components and actual pipeline entries.
JICA annual signature lists are a better cohort lead than its planned-date XML.
USAID access is demonstrated, but the acquired XML is an older administrative
slice, not a project-level comparison pool.

Next acceptance tests and ordering are specified in the round-3 memo: AFD
missing-ID/date reconciliation first; KfW reporting-period and FCDO documentary
pilots alongside; JICA older annual cohort; USAID identity/granularity before
large extraction. The delivered populations are not added across institutions.
No design or single lifecycle outcome is selected. Explicit disclosure, version
and unit checks are required before these observations enter the paper or site.

